[52.644739, "o", " ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ The bundled wheel is there. The issue is that `ensurepip` thinks pip is a…\r\n[approval] bash: rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info && python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm -rf /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip-24.3.1.dist-info &&…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Now let me verify it works:\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 && pip3 --version 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip --version 2>&1 && pip3 --version 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Let me do a quick test install to confirm it fully works:\r\n[approval] bash: python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash python3 -m pip install --dry-run requests 2>&1\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 16 tool call(s) so far\r\nEverything is working now.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong:** Your Python 3.1"]
[52.644761, "o", "3 installation (at `/usr/local`) had its `pip` module files deleted — only the `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata directory remained in `site-packages`. This is a classic case of pip's own files being removed (e.g., via `pip uninstall pip` or manual deletion), leaving behind only the metadata. Without the actual `pip` package code, `import pip` fails with `ModuleNotFoundError`.\r\n\r\n**What I did:** Removed the stale `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` directory and re-ran `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade`, which restored pip from the bundled wheel. Both `python3 -m pip` and `pip3` now work correctly (pip 24.3.1, Python 3.13).\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=implementor model=qwen3.6-35b tools=16 surface=b939125f62be\r\n"]
